RANGE 849-866
849:     We define a representation of multiplicative p-adics to be a pair a = (x, n) ∈
850: K ∗ ×Z≥0 such that x is HNF-reduced modulo pmax(1,n)+ordp (x) (cf. Section 3.1).
851: The open and closed subset xUpn of Kp∗ is called the represented subset of a and
852: denoted by R(a). We refer to x as the center of a, denoted c(a), and we call n
853: the precision of a, denoted p(a). All α ∈ R(a) have the same valuation at p and
854: we call this unique valuation the valuation of a, denoted ordp (a). Note that a is
855: not uniquely determined by its represented subset: if n = 0, there exist N (p)−1
856: representations b of multiplicative p-adics such that R(a) = R(b), where N (p)
857: denotes the norm of p. The set of representations of multiplicative p-adics is
858: denoted R(Kp∗ ).
859:     For any (x, n) ∈ K ∗ × Z≥0 we define the representation of multiplicative p-
860: adics associated to (x, n) to be the a ∈ R(Kp∗ ) with precision n and with center
861: the HNF-reduction of x modulo pmax(1,n)+ordp (x) .
862: 
863: Arithmetic. Let a, b ∈ R(Kp∗ ). We define the product ab of a and b to be the
864: representation of multiplicative p-adics associated to
865: 
866:                            (c(a)c(b), min(p(a), p(b))).
RANGE 1525-1538
1525:     We call a and b loosely equivalent if R(a) ∩ R(b) 6= ∅, i.e. there exists an
1526:      element α ∈ A that a and b both represent.
1527: Strict equivalence implies represented subset equivalence and represented subset
1528: equivalence implies loose equivalence. Strict and represented subset equivalence
1529: define equivalence relations, while loose equivalence fails to be transitive in gen-
1530: eral. The implementations of R(R) and R(C) in SageMath use strict equivalence
1531: for the == operator. On the other hand, the implementation of p-adic numbers
1532: in SageMath, for p a prime number, uses loose equivalence for comparison us-
1533: ing ==. Hence a consistent choice throughout SageMath was not possible. We
1534: did however want a single choice for all our own representations. When working
1535: with these representations during development, the question whether or not two
1536: representations could represent the same element came up more often than the
1537: question whether or not they certainly did represent the same element. Hence
1538: we ended up implementing loose equivalence for == comparison of our own rep-
